! v0.74.0 PCM test 2:  F- anion in water (HF/STO-3G, IEFPCM).
!
! Gauss-law check for IEFPCM:  for an isolated anion in a high-eps
! solvent, the apparent surface charge should approach
!   sum(q) = -Q_solute * (eps - 1) / eps
! with Q_solute = -1 here, so the predicted sum(q) is +0.98724 at
! eps = 78.36.
!
! C-PCM (v0.73.0) gave sum(q) = +0.990424 on this geometry.  IEFPCM
! at high eps should give a similar number (both reduce to the same
! conductor limit as eps -> infty).  Tolerance: |sum(q) - 0.987| < 0.05.
%method  HF
%basis   sto-3g
%charge  -1
%mult    1
%job     energy
%pcm     iefpcm
%solvent water
%geom angstrom
F  0.000000   0.000000   0.000000
*end
